25:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-8 xl:flex-row xl:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-w-0 flex-1 space-y-10","children":[["$","section",null,{"className":"max-w-3xl space-y-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-xl font-semibold","children":["How to use ","UUID Generator"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground md:text-base","children":"A UUID (Universally Unique Identifier) is a 128-bit value written as 32 hexadecimal characters in five groups, such as 3f2b8c1e-9a47-4d2f-b6a1-7c0e5d9a1b24. Developers use UUIDs as database keys, request IDs, file names and tokens because the chance of two random UUIDs matching is negligible. This tool generates version 4 (random) UUIDs instantly in your browser, one at a time or in a batch, ready to copy."}]]}],[["$","div","How to use the UUID generator",{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-display text-lg font-semibold","children":"How to use the UUID generator"}],[["$","p","Press “Generate 1” for a single UUID, or “Genera",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground md:text-base","children":"Press “Generate 1” for a single UUID, or “Generate 5” or “Generate 10” to create a batch. Press “Copy all” to copy every value on the screen, one per line, and paste them into your code, a spreadsheet, a test fixture or a configuration file."}],["$","p","Each press produces fresh random values. They ar",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground md:text-base","children":"Each press produces fresh random values. They are created locally with your browser’s cryptographic random source, so they are not sent to or logged by a server."}]],["$","ul",null,{"className":"list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","li","Generate 1, 5 or 10 UUIDs at a time",{"children":"Generate 1, 5 or 10 UUIDs at a time"}],["$","li","Use Copy all and paste the values where you need IDs",{"children":"Use Copy all and paste the values where you need IDs"}],["$","li","Lowercase hexadecimal is the usual format; most systems accept it as is",{"children":"Lowercase hexadecimal is the usual format; most systems accept it as is"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex justify-center py-2","children":["$","aside",null,{"aria-label":"Advertisement","data-cv-ad-zone":"mrec","className":"mx-auto flex w-fit max-w-full flex-col items-center","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1.5 text-center text-[9px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-muted-foreground/70","children":"Ad"}],["$","$L2a",null,{"slotKey":"inArticle","width":300,"height":250}]]}]}]]}],["$","div","What a version 4 UUID looks like",{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-display text-lg font-semibold","children":"What a version 4 UUID looks like"}],[["$","p","A UUID has the form 8-4-4-4-12 hexadecimal digit",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground md:text-base","children":"A UUID has the form 8-4-4-4-12 hexadecimal digits. In version 4 the first digit of the third group is always 4, and the first digit of the fourth group is 8, 9, a or b. Those fixed positions mark the version and variant, and the remaining 122 bits are random."}],["$","p","With 122 random bits, the number of possible UUI",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground md:text-base","children":"With 122 random bits, the number of possible UUIDs is about 5.3 × 10^36. Even if you generated a billion per second for decades, a collision would be extremely unlikely, which is why many systems can generate IDs independently without coordinating."}]],null,null]}],"$L2b","$L2c"]]}],"$L2d","$L2e","$L2f",null,"$L30"]}],"$L31"]}]
